WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.020
&gt;&gt; Thank you all

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:06.785
for coming for today's
distinguished lecture.

00:00:06.785 --> 00:00:09.630
It is a week

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.310
of attrition since AAAI

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:12.500
is happening at the same time,

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:13.870
WSDM is happening
at the same time.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:17.650
Eric Horvitz said Triple A.
Susan Dumais said Wizdom,

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:18.750
Harry Shum said Wizdom.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.030
So yeah. It's awesome
that [inaudible]

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:24.160
is building 99 has come on down.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.660
Are there any announcements
before we get started?

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:34.450
Okay. So, it's a privilege

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.840
to host Eva Tardos for
the distinguished lecture today.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.565
Eva is a professor at Cornell
and she was chair at 2010.

00:00:42.565 --> 00:00:48.145
I remember for sure. And
she's won so many awards.

00:00:48.145 --> 00:00:50.890
I noticed the bio
is not yet updated.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:52.290
So she's won things
like the Fulkerson

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.565
Prize, the Gödel Prize,

00:00:53.565 --> 00:00:55.695
but the EATCS has not yet

00:00:55.695 --> 00:00:58.955
up on the website or it's
not showing up on the bio.

00:00:58.955 --> 00:01:02.160
She won EATCS last year.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:07.135
And she's done foundational
work in algorithms,

00:01:07.135 --> 00:01:09.310
most famous for network flow.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:13.040
I know the first
computer textbook

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.335
I picked up was
Kleinberg and Tardos.

00:01:15.335 --> 00:01:17.790
The first time I actually

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.680
saw algorithms named after
people and things like that.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.630
You know like [inaudible]
there was the Lovász number.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.685
And there was
the Tardos function.

00:01:24.685 --> 00:01:26.685
And I was like, "No.

00:01:26.685 --> 00:01:32.810
I'm going to go to AI instead."

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:34.440
And also the first book I picked

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.455
up in grad school was
Algorithmic Game Theory.

00:01:36.455 --> 00:01:40.215
So she's played a foundational
role in that field.

00:01:40.215 --> 00:01:43.230
Yeah. Her contributions
are too numerous to list

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.060
but one thing I did
realize in reading

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.930
through her bio
is it's definitely

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.350
incomplete because
it does not make

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:53.735
any mention of
her unwavering effort

00:01:53.735 --> 00:01:56.280
at a diversity and

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.650
inclusion and stem outreach
and things like that.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.720
I think the first
email I got from

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.695
you was the Expanding
Your Horizons email,

00:02:03.695 --> 00:02:06.760
encouraging grad
students to go and

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:10.175
teach those in middle schools
and things like that.

00:02:10.175 --> 00:02:12.225
So yeah.

00:02:12.225 --> 00:02:14.735
It's an honor to have,

00:02:14.735 --> 00:02:16.000
Eva, come here to talk today.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.240
I'll handle it over to her.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:20.920
&gt;&gt; Thank you very
much. Thank you Hadid.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:27.245
Thank you all for coming and I

00:02:27.245 --> 00:02:31.755
hope you'll find
the discussion interesting.

00:02:31.755 --> 00:02:35.655
I guess one comment
on Hadid summary,

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:38.885
I hope you guys all do

00:02:38.885 --> 00:02:42.400
at improving diversity
and outreach.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.405
I think there's a lot. We
all collectively can do.

00:02:45.405 --> 00:02:49.410
I do not keep this kind of
things on my CV because I

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:53.240
guess I think of my CV as
a sort of professional,

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.080
sort of my science
contributions.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.750
It's not because I don't
think these are important.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:00.020
I think they're
very important and I

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:01.740
hope you guys all do this.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.090
In particular,
especially I mean,

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:07.085
the people who are not
minority and women.

00:03:07.085 --> 00:03:09.860
Thank you all.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.840
This is an effort we
should all participate

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:16.625
in especially the
non-minorities.

00:03:16.625 --> 00:03:18.830
And I hope you guys care.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.560
Anyway, but I will limit

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:26.345
my discussion to
the particular topic.

00:03:26.345 --> 00:03:30.070
So the thing I want
to talk about today is

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:35.480
how I think about learning in
the context of Game Theory.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:41.135
And I guess it really sort
of come in kind of two parts.

00:03:41.135 --> 00:03:45.250
Our people learning in
a setting where there is

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:50.485
games and if I can
assume that the learning,

00:03:50.485 --> 00:03:53.150
what kind of things
can I say about what

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:55.630
happens in games if people were

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:57.450
nice enough to actually play

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.760
in this relatively wise manner

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.200
where I have to be
careful on what I

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.675
really mean whether it's
wise to learn or not.

00:04:04.675 --> 00:04:07.195
And that's what I'm going
to really start with.

00:04:07.195 --> 00:04:10.870
So that's the efficiency
part is what can I

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:12.020
say about the outcome of

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:15.075
a game or efficiency
of art compart.

00:04:15.075 --> 00:04:18.210
And then, the first part
is learning in games.

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:21.230
So what do I mean by games?

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:25.620
Unfortunate. I thought
I had a forwarder,

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:30.705
but apparently do not.

00:04:30.705 --> 00:04:38.100
What I mean is a game of
some sort where some players,

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:40.100
which in depends on

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:44.540
the application that
particular player can be either

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:50.380
the people driving
cars or it could

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:56.740
be rerouters routing traffic.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:01.060
Some algorithm, can be
a human or an algorithm,

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:05.580
is making decisions as you
go one decision at a time

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.660
trying to repeatedly optimize
some objective function.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.590
To make it more mathematical,

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:14.630
I will assume that there

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:17.525
are a couple of things that
are written on the slide.

00:05:17.525 --> 00:05:20.635
The example here is
a Congestion Game.

00:05:20.635 --> 00:05:23.225
So I guess the assumption that

00:05:23.225 --> 00:05:26.890
cars or [inaudible] try
to follow a shortest path.

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:28.550
They try to figure
out where they get to

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:31.470
the destination of the fastest.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.735
Of course this is a game.

00:05:33.735 --> 00:05:35.970
Congestion. Your speed depends

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:37.680
on who else is going
on the same path,

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.150
whether your current traffic.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:42.585
But there is an implicit
assumption in here,

00:05:42.585 --> 00:05:48.130
is that you're not trying
to hurt other people,

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:50.690
your goal isn't
that other people

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:51.920
don't get to their destination.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:53.935
You just want to get
to your destination.

00:05:53.935 --> 00:05:56.380
And then I guess
one part that will come

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:58.700
in certainly in our results,

00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:00.685
or I think what's important to

00:06:00.685 --> 00:06:03.900
think about in
the context of games.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.300
I think I'm giving up on this,

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.800
is that the traffic

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:12.255
isn't as stable necessarily
as you can hope.

00:06:12.255 --> 00:06:15.950
Depending on whether
the Superbowl ended,

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:20.240
or some new site and then on
the New York Times website,

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.210
or whatever happened,
traffic does change.

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:27.965
And so, as you return even you
optimally send traffic,

00:06:27.965 --> 00:06:29.620
you're going to have to learn,

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:33.125
or adjust to changing environment
and that's going to be

00:06:33.125 --> 00:06:37.400
an important feature as I
get later into the talk.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:39.150
I will alternate between

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.110
using traffic routing
in my example,

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:43.585
or actually using
auction as my example.

00:06:43.585 --> 00:06:46.910
Where auction I think of
mostly advertisement auction.

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:49.085
I need to think of a setup

00:06:49.085 --> 00:06:51.535
that's where learning
will be relevant,

00:06:51.535 --> 00:06:53.800
where we repeating a game

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:55.950
such as the
advertisement auction,

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:57.490
or such as traffic routing,

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:59.890
where the individual outcomes in

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:02.665
a game are not very important.

00:07:02.665 --> 00:07:04.570
Like in ad auctions,

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:06.575
a particular value of one ad,

00:07:06.575 --> 00:07:08.825
it's a tiny fraction of a cent.

00:07:08.825 --> 00:07:11.000
In traffic routing,

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:14.600
car I think they might not
apply so we'll but certainly,

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.650
in packet traffic, if
one packet goes bad,

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.190
the internet drops
it and tries it

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:20.780
again, not that big a deal.

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:22.590
If a host chime of
packet does back,

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:24.685
that's kind of a problem.
It's a big problem.

00:07:24.685 --> 00:07:26.310
Similarly, in ad auction,

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:29.325
if your whole campaign tanks
that's a problem,

00:07:29.325 --> 00:07:30.860
if one ad didn't work out,

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:32.705
oh well, one it doesn't matter.

00:07:32.705 --> 00:07:34.370
So, that's the kind of

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:36.600
setup we're learning
will make a lot of sense.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:41.590
And here again, the
advertisers not very fast,

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:43.160
but can leave and
join the system,

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:44.360
and there is an ecosystem

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:46.665
where things can
change as we go.

00:07:46.665 --> 00:07:49.520
So, to make it
slightly mathematical,

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:51.640
I guess the game will be that

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:54.050
their auctions that
people can take,

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:56.475
put up ads, choose
a rat to drive.

00:07:56.475 --> 00:07:58.160
And then, something
happens and you

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:00.090
repeat this action over time.

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:04.630
And the assumptions are players

00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:06.810
somehow take
the past data and figure

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:09.330
out from the past data
what the hell to do next.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.120
So that's the model of learning.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:14.665
I assume that the goal is,

00:08:14.665 --> 00:08:17.140
that they try to
an average do well.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:19.370
Again, not very
sensitive to outliers,

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:22.080
but most of the time
should do well.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:28.600
And that's because small value
of cost in each period,

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:30.150
so exploration is a good way

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:32.050
to figure out
what the hell to do.

00:08:32.050 --> 00:08:34.550
And again, the idea
would be that in

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:35.720
the beginning they
don't know what's

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:38.580
up and then later on they know.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:43.400
So I guess, that's
the main topic of

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:45.435
the sort of repeated games

00:08:45.435 --> 00:08:47.700
and what the hell happens
in repeated games.

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.310
And I want to start
with something

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:51.120
which given that this
is the AI colloquial

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:55.285
maybe I shouldn't
spend too much time

00:08:55.285 --> 00:09:00.240
on of why do we
want to do learning?

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.040
And what might be
the goal of learning?

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.485
I want to start with actually,

00:09:04.485 --> 00:09:07.260
learning and games was
a topic combination

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:09.300
that we weren't
the first to start.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.100
Economists started it before

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:13.740
AI was really
such a dominant field.

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:16.190
So, they were asking

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:17.930
this question and
not the question

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:19.410
that I might want to ask,

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:22.350
they asked this question
or learning is cool,

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:25.370
because players might learn
to find the Nash Equilibrium,

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:28.160
we'll just figure out
what mode of the learning is.

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:29.710
They weren't
actually thinking of

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:31.275
the kind of game
I'm thinking of,

00:09:31.275 --> 00:09:34.500
which we naturally
or have to play with

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:35.955
small six day instead

00:09:35.955 --> 00:09:38.580
thinking of what
they called pre-play.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.440
So, they were
thinking of there's

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:42.325
a giant enormous
auction going on,

00:09:42.325 --> 00:09:46.800
think of the FCC spectrum
auction that just happened.

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:50.305
But before it happened, for
the previous couple of years,

00:09:50.305 --> 00:09:52.875
there were all kinds of
training things going on,

00:09:52.875 --> 00:09:56.085
to train the auctioneer's
how to bid in this game.

00:09:56.085 --> 00:09:57.700
So, they were
thinking, let's think

00:09:57.700 --> 00:09:59.400
of this training as a learning.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:02.750
If you're on the training
before the real auction then,

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:05.070
the players might,
via the learning,

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:07.080
figure out what
the Nash Equilibrium is.

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:09.090
And then, when
the real auction comes,

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.910
they can play the Nash
Equilibrium. That was the goal.

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:13.430
So, they were thinking of

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:16.015
learning just the way
we're thinking of learning,

00:10:16.015 --> 00:10:17.970
but they were hoping that

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:20.370
learning will find
the Nash Equilibrium and that's

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:22.810
basically was what
they hoped and

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:25.820
just to realize how
early they started.

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:30.030
Robinson in 51, saw about
fictitious play which

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:34.385
apparently means
what I said there,

00:10:34.385 --> 00:10:36.390
your best response
to the past history.

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:38.425
So you watch what
happened in the past,

00:10:38.425 --> 00:10:40.720
you think of past history as

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:43.750
a sample distribution of
what other people are doing,

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:45.220
assuming they're going to sample

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:46.820
the very same distribution,

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:48.450
and just best respond to that.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:51.445
This is a sort of bayesian
style learning assumption,

00:10:51.445 --> 00:10:53.420
that the previous behavior is

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:54.890
a bayesian sample from something

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:56.910
and you react to
that bayesian sample.

00:10:56.910 --> 00:11:02.330
And that's what Robinson
was studying and she proved,

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:06.445
by the way, just because of
Odette's comment, she proved.

00:11:06.445 --> 00:11:08.175
Robinson was a woman.

00:11:08.175 --> 00:11:13.310
That this pre-play was
hoping to learn lead to

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:15.780
a Nash and what was hoped

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:17.060
and that's what she was studying

00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:18.620
what time does it lead to Nash?

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:20.470
And what do I mean
by lead to a Nash?

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:23.265
I hope that maybe it doesn't
need a definition either,

00:11:23.265 --> 00:11:29.150
but just in case,
oops, what did I do?

00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:38.185
What it means that they will
find a solution that stable.

00:11:38.185 --> 00:11:40.585
Stable in the following sense,

00:11:40.585 --> 00:11:43.690
that stable as a one-shot game.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:45.600
If I take and there is

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:47.565
a slight awkwardness in

00:11:47.565 --> 00:11:49.100
alternating between routing and

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:50.720
auction as my two examples,

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:52.560
then one is an example where we

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:55.240
minimizing the delays
some sort of cost.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:57.060
The other one is an example of

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:59.290
everyone maximize our utilities.

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:02.030
When we want to
minimize delays then,

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:03.880
Nash solution a, this is

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:07.750
a vector with AI is
the component for player i.

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:09.925
Then, this is what
it means to be Nash.

00:12:09.925 --> 00:12:11.740
For every player i,

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:13.750
if they choose to
do something else

00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:16.475
instead of their
own component AI,

00:12:16.475 --> 00:12:18.685
they choose something x instead

00:12:18.685 --> 00:12:20.160
that would have a higher cost.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:22.210
That is they don't want
to do this other thing.

00:12:22.210 --> 00:12:23.680
That's what it means to be Nash.

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:27.190
And again, the notation
here is a minus i is a n

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:28.855
minus one dimensional
vector with

00:12:28.855 --> 00:12:31.260
the i component missing,

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:32.980
which got replaced by x.

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:35.165
And that's what it
means to be Nash.

00:12:35.165 --> 00:12:38.080
And what they hoped
is somehow learning

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:41.730
will either achieve this
one-shot game Nash Equilibrium.

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:44.105
So that would be
an equilibrium verdict,

00:12:44.105 --> 00:12:46.305
repeatedly play the same thing
over and over again,

00:12:46.305 --> 00:12:48.140
or at least converge to it in

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:51.480
some sort of distance
like metric distance.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:53.930
And I guess, we would call this

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:55.920
as a no regret assumption.

00:12:55.920 --> 00:13:00.625
And the question
was, does fictitious

00:13:00.625 --> 00:13:05.090
Play, that is best responding
to the past history,

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:06.830
leads to Nash equilibrium.

